{"name":"Cactus Green","hex":"#56603d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cactus","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any member of the family Cactaceae, a family of flowering New World succulent plants suited to a hot, semi-desert climate.","Any succulent plant with a thick fleshy stem bearing spines but no leaves, such as euphorbs."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Non-functional, broken, exhausted, dead."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cactus"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cactus","blocks":[{"text":"A cactus is a member of the plant family Cactaceae, a family of the order Caryophyllales comprising about 131 genera with some 1,866 known species. The word cactus derives, through Latin, from the Ancient Greek word κάκτος (káktos), a name originally used by Theophrastus for a spiny plant whose identity is now not certain. Cacti occur in a wide range of shapes and sizes. They are native to the Americas, ranging from Patagonia in the south to parts of western Canada in the north, with the exception of Rhipsalis baccifera, which is also found in Africa and Sri Lanka. Cacti are adapted to live in very dry environments, including the Atacama Desert, one of the driest places on Earth. Because of this, cacti show many adaptations to conserve water. For example, almost all cacti are succulents, meaning they have thickened, fleshy organs adapted to store water. Unlike many other succulents, the stem is the only part of most cacti where water storage takes place. Most species of cacti have lost true leaves, retaining only spines, which are highly modified leaves. As well as defending against herbivores, spines help prevent water loss by reducing air flow close to the cactus and providing some shade. In the absence of true leaves, cacti's enlarged stems carry out photosynthesis."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Various_Cactaceae.jpg/330px-Various_Cactaceae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cactus"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Singapore_Botanic_Gardens_Cactus_Garden_2.jpg/500px-Singapore_Botanic_Gardens_Cactus_Garden_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Singapore_Botanic_Gardens_Cactus_Garden_2.jpg/1280px-Singapore_Botanic_Gardens_Cactus_Garden_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Singapore_Botanic_Gardens_Cactus_Garden_2.jpg/500px-Singapore_Botanic_Gardens_Cactus_Garden_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASingapore_Botanic_Gardens_Cactus_Garden_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Cactus1web.jpg/500px-Cactus1web.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Cactus1web.jpg/500px-Cactus1web.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACactus1web.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Barrel_cactus_with_a_view.JPG/500px-Barrel_cactus_with_a_view.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Barrel_cactus_with_a_view.JPG/1280px-Barrel_cactus_with_a_view.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Barrel_cactus_with_a_view.JPG/500px-Barrel_cactus_with_a_view.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ferocactus species, a cactoid, in its native Arizona habitat","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABarrel_cactus_with_a_view.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Arrojadoa_luetzelburgii_4.jpeg/500px-Arrojadoa_luetzelburgii_4.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Arrojadoa_luetzelburgii_4.jpeg/1280px-Arrojadoa_luetzelburgii_4.jpe